  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest radar and satellite imagery reveal a large area of \nclouds and generally light rain showers across the Upper Ohio \nValley and Lower Great Lakes this afternoon, associated with a \nsurface trough ahead of a broad upper-level feature to the west \nacross the Upper Midwest. Surface observations also depict a \nstationary front located further south near the Lower Ohio \nValley, thus thunder chances for our area through Wednesday will\nremain limited. Occasional rain showers, particularly across \nportions of North-Central and Northeast Ohio, will continue into\nthis evening, before largely dissipating overnight. Can't rule \nout rain persisting across a small portion of Northwest Ohio \novernight into Tuesday along the surface trough, though coverage\nshould be more isolated and light elsewhere. The next chance \nfor widespread and perhaps a more steady rain may return late \nTuesday night into Wednesday as the upper-level feature \napproaches the area, with an increasing arc of 850 mb E to SE \nwinds transporting more favorable moisture into the area. \n\nPeriodic rain showers and cloud cover will contribute to below \naverage daytime temperatures in the 60s or perhaps even upper 50s at \ntimes and lows in the 50s or upper 40s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure will build south across the Great Lakes by the \nend of the week and likely persist through the weekend. Initially,\nhigh pressure and the departing chilly air mass could result in\na favorable scenario for some lows in the upper 30s to develop,\nparticularly across inland Northwest Pennsylvania, though 40s \nshould be more common elsewhere. The air mass will gradually \nmoderate through the weekend with high pressure in place as \ntemperatures return to the upper 60s to low 70s by Sunday.",
